{"type":"text","timestamp":1792512247906,"sessionID":"ses_ea5f9afedffeff3jS3ECGmAlz5","part":{"id":"prt_15a0e42c4001zRrRaHt2EsffAM","messageID":"msg_15a0e2535001I9p9IFxhBYrVZl","sessionID":"ses_ea5f9afedffeff3jS3ECGmAlz5","type":"text","text":"\n\nLet me verify my no-op logic against the invariant before running. The key claim: after a no-op update, the *same* certificate (parent/discover/finish) must still be valid for the *new* graph. For insert, I only skip recompute when the new edge is a back edge or forward edge (both legal, so the old certificate stays valid). For delete, I only skip when the edge is a non-tree edge (removing it can't create a forward-cross edge or disconnect the tree). Let me run check 2:\n\n","time":{"start":1792512246468,"end":1792512247900}}}
